,4.2]},{"tree":[40,-40,12,4.2]},{"tree":[70,-40,12,4.2]},{"tree":[96,-40,12,4.2]}],"notes":"Parrish Art Museum, Water Mill (Herzog and de Meuron, 2012): a single long building, about 187 m by 29 m, under twin barn-like gabled roofs of corrugated galvanised metal that overhang the white-painted concrete walls to make porches down both long sides, with slim posts; long stretches of glass at the entrance, caf\u00e9 and studios, skylights along the north slopes; set in a meadow. Turned 16 degrees north of east to lie along its axis in the ground file. Written from general knowledge; guessed: eave and ridge heights (about 5 and 10 m), the skylight rhythm, where the glass runs. Refined 6 Oct 2026 from published descriptions and the ground's OpenStreetMap outline, not photographs: the outline is the whole long building, lying 13.5 degrees north of east, so the model was turned 2.5 degrees further toward it and moved 6 m north, after which it covers the outline almost exactly; the meadow's edges were given their hedgerows - a line of trees along the Montauk Highway side to the south and a few at the north edge. Still guessed: eave and ridge heights, the skylight rhythm, where the glass runs, the parking's shape."}